# One Windows taskkill boundary control

The first v5 package has a descendant-marker failure, but its old fixture discards
taskkill output and birth identities. One separate bounded synthetic control will
capture those missing diagnostics. It cannot pass the old test or close package
admission. The original watchdog, 0.35-second test timeout and assertions stay
unchanged; no unchanged-source package or old fixture is repeated.

The control creates one root and one leaf using the current Python image under
one exclusive repository-target directory. Both self-exit within eight seconds
of their own start. The leaf's marker delay stays 1.5 seconds. An explicit release
file permits earlier cooperative cleanup. No host service, clock, server, product
workload, trust enrollment or production writer is touched.

Before the single termination call, the existing read-only OwnedObserver and
HeldLineage bind live handles to the root and leaf, matching parent snapshots,
full images and creation identities. Their source digests and the original
taskkill binary digest are fixed. Taskkill is invoked exactly once with the
original `/PID ... /T /F` arguments against only that enrolled root. Complete
stdout/stderr and return code are retained. No process-census-based second kill
or fixture retry is allowed.

The 0.35-second delay starts after enrollment. This diagnostic deliberately
characterizes an already observed tree, not the old launch-relative deadline.
Its result must not be substituted for that original test. Taskkill has a
five-second subprocess bound; observation follows for two seconds. Cleanup
releases the fixture, reaps its root and waits on the same held objects, checking
their original creation identities. A remaining process or missing identity is
a retained refusal, not a retirement pass inferred from PID absence.

The target-only coordinator is retained with its first intent, streams, metadata
and result. A successful diagnostic capture is distinct from successful taskkill,
passing the original watchdog, proving arbitrary writer-alias cessation or
qualifying production Windows/Linux execution. No numeric performance claim or
timeout relaxation follows, whichever outcome is observed.
